Versions of S-PLUS before version 7, and the Professional Developer edition
of S-PLUS 7.0, are in-memory applications.  Simply put, this means that
S-PLUS must keep copies of your data, and any intermediate copies of the data
used when preparing or analyzing it, all in memory at once. (Depending on the
type of calculation, you might need 5 or more copies.)  If you don't have
enough memory (physical or virtual available), you'll get a "cannot allocate
dynamic memory" error.

With S-PLUS 7 Professional, you'll get a warning if you import a data file
larger than an arbitrary threshold, to alert you to this possibility.  It's
just a warning though, and you're free to continue to import and analyze the
data, provided you have enough memory available to do your computations.

If you're working with large data sets, you might want to consider upgrading
to S-PLUS 7 Enterprise Developer.  Unlike the Professional Developer edition,
and all editions prior to version 7, this edition incorporates a pipeline
archicture which does not require multiple copies of the data to be kept in
memory.  This means you can analyze very large (multi-gigabyte) data sets,
even if you don't have a large amount of RAM.

You can read about the additional capabilities of S-PLUS 7 Enterprise
Developer at:

   http://www.insightful.com/products/splus/s7_features.asp

and this white paper contains detailed information about its pipeline
architecture:

   http://www.insightful.com/insightful_doclib/document.asp?id=167

Hope this helps,
